From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 08:45:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 34/64] fix(gastown): stop a parked work bead shadowing the
 patrol wisp at startup (gcp-tl8)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The refinery and deacon Startup Step 1 blocks were captioned "check for an
in-progress patrol wisp" but ran `{{ .AssignedInProgressQuery }}` — gc's generic
"what is in_progress on my hook" expansion, which filters on status and assignee
only and truncates at `--limit=1`. It answers a different question than the
caption asks: it returns the first in-progress bead of *any* type assigned to the
agent, and that single row is all the agent sees.

Observed live on the gascity-packs refinery: work bead gcp-xbl is held
in_progress under a standing do-not-merge order, so the shipped probe returned
that bug rather than `gcp-wisp-jv2`, the real `mol-refinery-patrol` wisp. Two
failures follow. The agent "resumes" formula steps against a bead that carries no
formula, and because it believes it already holds a wisp it never pours one — so
the patrol silently stops advancing. When the parked bead is blocked by design
the condition is permanent, not transient.

gcp-xbl and gcp-ah8h fixed the sibling `--type=molecule` queries (adding
`--include-infra`, widening `--status`). Neither touches this one: there is no
`--type` filter here to repair, because the line is a gc-provided expansion with
no type filter at all. Replaced at both sites with the same resolver the witness
prompt and the patrol formulas already use —
`--status=open,in_progress --type=molecule --include-infra --limit=0` — where
`--type=molecule` is what structurally prevents a work bead from being read as
the wisp, and `--limit=0` stops the truncation that hid the real wisp below the
cut. The three patrol-role blocks in propulsion.template.md carried the same
mislabelled query in prose and are updated to match; the mayor, crew and dog
blocks in that file keep the generic query, which asks it the question it does
answer.

Two incidental corrections in the deacon block, both required for the fix to
hold rather than cleanup: the cold-start pour is now guarded by `if [ -z "$WISP" ]`
(it previously ran unconditionally, so every restart poured a duplicate), and it
assigns with `$GC_AGENT` instead of `$GC_ALIAS` so the pour and the probe agree
on one identity. The refinery prompt already documents why: `$GC_ALIAS` can be
empty or stale, which is how a refinery once self-polled for 13h42m with seven
queued beads.

Guarded in tests/test_wisp_molecule_queries.py, which gcp-xbl added but which
asserted only on lines that already had a `--type` flag — the shape here has none,
so it is guarded structurally instead: patrol-role prompt assets must not reach
for the generic hook query at all. Scoping propulsion.template.md needs
per-`{{ define }}` attribution, and the block scanner depth-counts
`if`/`range`/`with`/`block` alongside `define` so a nested action cannot end a
role block early and silently drop every line after it. Both facets have negative
tests.

Validated: tests/test_wisp_molecule_queries.py 7 passed; go vet, go build,
go test -race, golangci-lint (0 issues) and validate_registry.py all clean.
tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py::test_shipped_pack_assets_route_beads_commands_through_gc
fails on this branch and equally on a stashed-clean checkout of the same base —
pre-existing, untouched by this diff, filed separately.

